course: everyday
lesson: 519
title: Parents far away: the army, work abroad, prison
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some parents are alive and loving and still far away: in the army, working abroad, or in prison. Cody's dad is a soldier on a long posting. They FaceTime on Sundays when the signal works, Dad's face freezing mid-laugh. On sports day all the dads are lined up by the rope except his. Cody wins the 60 metres and looks straight at the empty spot. His mum filmed the whole race, and Dad watches it three times that night: «Proper rapid, son!» At Cody's school there's a club for forces kids, where everybody knows what an empty spot feels like.
explain: Jaya's dad works in Dubai to earn more for the family. He sends money and presents: a tablet, gold earrings, sweets. «I'd rather have him than presents,» Jaya mutters. She tells him on the phone, and he says, «Beta, I'll prepone my flight» («beta» is a Hindi word parents use for their child; «prepone» is Indian English for move earlier). He comes home a week early. Sami lives with his dad and gran; his mum works as a nurse abroad and sends him a voice note every day. He listens before bed, every night, even the boring ones about the weather.
explain: Some jobs mean coming and going. Oisín's da works on an oil rig: two weeks away, two weeks home. When he's away the house is quiet; when he's back it's loud. «Ah, the craic is back,» Mam says («craic» is Irish for fun and chat). Mia-Rose's mum is on a Navy ship for six months, and Mia-Rose crosses off the days on the calendar. Lily-Jo's dad is FIFO, fly-in fly-out: three weeks at a mine in Western Australia, one at home. «It's like he's a visitor,» she says. They talk it through, and now on his first day home they always go to the servo (petrol station) for a pie, just the two of them. Bryce's dad came home from his deployment with a hurt leg. He can't shoot hoops like before, and Bryce misses the old Dad. They're fixin' to (about to) start chess instead. It isn't the same. It's still something.
explain: When a parent is in prison, children miss them and may feel ashamed, but it is not their fault. Ellis visits his mam once a month. There are guards, lockers and plastic chairs bolted to the floor. Mam hugs him for as long as she's allowed. He loves the hug and hates the place. Tegan-Rae writes her dad letters about school; he writes back with drawings of her dog. At school a kid sneers at Dakota, «Your dad's a criminal, that's well sus» («sus» = dodgy). Dakota says, «He made a mistake. He's still my dad.» Then she tells her teacher. UK charities like Children Heard and Seen help children with a parent in prison.
explain: Grown-ups sometimes hide prison to protect children. Dayo was told his dad was «working away». Then he overheard his aunties talking. He felt lied to and furious. His mum sat down with him: «I was scared of hurting you. I got it wrong. Ask me anything.» It took weeks before Dayo stopped being angry. Being told the truth gently is usually easier than finding out by accident.
fact: Some parents live far away because they are in the armed forces, work abroad or in another town, or are in prison.
fact: A parent who is far away is still a parent; calls, voice notes, letters and videos keep families close.
fact: Children of soldiers and sailors often feel proud and sad at once; many schools have clubs for forces children.
fact: Some parents work abroad to earn more for their family; children can tell them they miss them.
fact: Some jobs, such as on oil rigs, ships or fly-in fly-out mines, mean parents are away for weeks and then home for weeks.
fact: Small rituals for the days a parent comes home help them feel less like a visitor.
fact: When a parent is in prison, it is never the child's fault, and the parent is still their parent.
fact: Prison visits have guards and strict rules, but children can still talk and hug.
fact: Teasing a child about a parent in prison is bullying and should be told to a teacher.
fact: Being told the truth gently is usually easier than finding out by accident.
fact: An injury can change what a parent can do; families can find new things to do together.
fact: «Craic» is Irish for fun and chat, «prepone» is Indian English for move earlier, «servo» is Australian for petrol station, «sus» means dodgy, and «fixin' to» means about to.
exercise: How does Cody keep in touch with his soldier dad? | FaceTime on Sundays, when the signal works | Letters by pigeon every single Sunday afternoon | He doesn't, soldiers can't talk to their families | Only through his teacher, who passes on messages
exercise: Cody won the race and looked at the empty spot. How did he most likely feel? | Proud and lonely at once | Only happy | Only angry at his dad for missing it | Bored
exercise: What did Cody's mum do so Dad didn't miss the race? | Filmed it for him | Ran it for him | Asked them to wait | Cancelled sports day
exercise: Jaya says, «I'd rather have him than presents.» What does she mean? | Having Dad with her matters more than gifts | She doesn't like the tablet or the earrings | She wants even bigger presents from now on | She'd like Dad to stop sending her money
exercise: «I'll prepone my flight.» What does «prepone» mean? | Move it earlier | Cancel it | Move it to a later date | Pay for it
exercise: How does Sami's mum keep close from abroad? | A voice note every day | A parcel once a year | Nothing at all | Long emails to his teacher every week
exercise: «Ah, the craic is back.» What does Mam mean? | The fun and chat are back now Da's home | The kitchen floor has cracked again in the same place | Da has brought back a broken plate | The noise means someone's in trouble
exercise: How does Mia-Rose cope with Mum being on a ship for six months? | Crossing off days on the calendar | Refusing to talk about Mum at all | Asking to go on the ship as well | Pretending Mum's in the next room
exercise: Lily-Jo's dad is «FIFO». What is that? | He flies to work far away for weeks, then flies home | He's a famous footballer who plays abroad on Fridays | He works in a fish and chip shop on the night shift | He is in the army and goes where he's sent by boat
exercise: What helped Lily-Jo feel her dad wasn't just a visitor? | A pie at the servo on his first day home | Hiding from him for the first few days | Making him sleep in the spare room as a guest | Asking Mum to stop him going back to the mine
exercise: Bryce's dad can't shoot hoops since his injury. What are they doing instead? | Starting chess | Nothing, ever | Running races | Arguing about it
exercise: When a parent is in prison, whose fault is it? | Never the child's | The child's, partly | The other parent's | The teacher's
exercise: What is a prison visit like for Ellis? | Guards, plastic chairs, and a long hug from Mam | A sleepover in Mam's room with a film | A chat through a glass screen on the phone, with no hug | A trip to the beach with a guard watching
exercise: How do Tegan-Rae and her dad keep in touch? | Letters, and he draws her dog | Text messages every hour | Video calls every morning before school | They don't keep in touch
exercise: «Your dad's a criminal, that's well sus.» What did Dakota do? | Said he's still her dad, then told her teacher | Punched the kid hard and ran out of the school | Agreed her dad was bad and cried all day long | Said nothing and never came back to school
exercise: Dayo found out the truth by overhearing. What did his mum say? | She'd been scared of hurting him, she got it wrong, and he could ask anything | That he must never ever talk about it again with anyone at all | That he had heard it wrong and his dad was working away | That he should be cross with his aunties, not with her
test: «if ur dad's in prison ur gonna end up there too lol». True? | No, a parent's mistakes aren't the child's | Yes, it always runs in families | Yes, unless you move to a new town | No, because prisons don't take children's dads
test: Jaya: «he sends money so he must not wanna be here». What's more likely? | He works away to help the family and misses her too | He doesn't love her, or he would just stay at home | He likes Dubai better than his family and his house | He'd rather send presents than see her face at all
test: «army kids are always fine, they're used to it». Right? | No, they often feel proud and sad at once | Yes, they never miss their parents | Yes, because soldiers come home every night | No, army kids are never proud
test: What would most likely happen if nobody ever told Dayo the truth and he kept overhearing bits? | He'd feel more confused and lied to | He'd never find out anything at all | His dad would come home sooner | He would stop caring about his dad
test: In a film, the kid with a dad in prison is the baddie. What's more real? | Kids with a parent in prison are ordinary kids | Those kids are always trouble in real life | Only baddies have parents in prison | Films always show prison families correctly
test: Ellis: «hate that place, the chairs are bolted down and there's guards». Should he stop visiting? | Not if he wants to see Mam; the place is hard, the hug is real | Yes, children are never allowed to visit a prison | Yes, because it's the guards who decide who he loves | Yes, he should only write and never see her face
test: Bryce: «Dad's not the same, it's a bummer». What could help? | Finding new things to do together | Pretending the injury isn't there | Telling Dad he's useless now | Never playing anything again
test: ★★★ Oisín's da works two weeks on the rig, then two weeks home, starting on the rig on 1 March. Is he home or away on 20 March? | Home | Away | On the plane | Half and half
test: ★★★ Mia-Rose's mum sails for six months, leaving in early January. In which month should she be back? | July | June | August | December
test: ★★★ Lily-Jo's dad: three weeks at the mine, one week home, again and again. Out of 8 weeks, how many is he home? | 2 | 1 | 4 | 6
test: «my mate's mum is FIFO, does that mean she never comes home?». Right? | No, she flies home after her weeks away | Yes, she lives at the mine forever | Yes, FIFO means gone for good | No, she drives home from work every single night
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the position of a child whose parent is imprisoned? | The child bears no fault and the parent remains his or her parent | The child shares in the parent's guilt in equal measure | The child ceases to be regarded as the parent's child | The child is required to sever all contact with the parent
exam: Identify the principal reason, as stated in the lesson, for which certain parents undertake employment abroad. | To earn a greater income on behalf of the family | Because they no longer wish to see their children | Because employment within the country is prohibited | In order to avoid the celebration of family birthdays
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the disclosure of a parent's imprisonment to a child. | Gentle disclosure is usually easier than discovery by accident | Concealment is invariably preferable in every case | Discovery by accident is always the kinder course | Children must never be informed under any circumstances
exam: Given that a parent is employed on a fly-in fly-out basis, ascertain the pattern of the parent's presence. | Periods of several weeks away alternate with periods at home | The parent departs permanently and does not return | The parent returns home at the conclusion of every day | The parent is absent solely on Fridays of each week
